{
  "scenarioId": "field_coordinator_burden_001",
  "turns": [
    {
      "probe": "What percentage of humanitarian field coordinators' time is spent on data collection and reporting?",
      "text": "I could not find an exact standard or statistic in the Sphere Handbook, CHS, or IASC guidelines that specifies a precise percentage of time humanitarian field coordinators should spend on data collection and reporting. However, these activities are crucial for ensuring accountability, transparency, and effective response coordination.\n\nIn practice, the amount of time spent on data collection and reporting can vary widely depending on the context, scale of operations, and specific roles within an organization. It is common for humanitarian field coordinators to allocate a significant portion of their time—often more than 20% but less than 50%—to these activities, as they are essential for monitoring progress against objectives, reporting to donors, and informing decision-making processes.\n\nFor precise guidance tailored to your specific role or organization, I recommend consulting internal guidelines or speaking with a supervisor who can provide context-specific advice.",
